    __set_page_dirty() was moved to the callers.
   =20
    That removal generally didn't matter, since a filesystem would serial=
ize
    truncation (which clears the page mapping) against writing (which mar=
ks
    the buffer dirty), so locking at a higher level (either per-page or a=
n
    inode at a time) should mean that the buffer page would be stable.  A=
nd
    indeed, nothing bad seemed to happen.
   =20
    Except it turns out that apparently reiserfs does something odd when
