    x86: don't call '->send_IPI_mask()' with an empty mask
   =20
    As noted in 83d349f35e1ae72268c5104dbf9ab2ae635425d4 ("x86: don't sen=
d
    an IPI to the empty set of CPU's"), some APIC's will be very unhappy
    with an empty destination mask.  That commit added a WARN_ON() for th=
at
    case, and avoided the resulting problem, but didn't fix the underlyin=
g
    reason for why those empty mask cases happened.
   =20
    This fixes that, by checking the result of 'cpumask_andnot()' of the
    current CPU actually has any other CPU's left in the set of CPU's to =
be
    sent a TLB flush, and not calling down to the IPI code if the mask is
    empty.
   =20
    The reason this started happening at all is that we started passing j=
ust
    the CPU mask pointers around in commit 4595f9620 ("x86: change
    flush_tlb_others to take a const struct cpumask"), and when we did th=
at,
    the cpumask was no longer thread-local.
   =20
    Before that commit, flush_tlb_mm() used to create it's own copy of
    'mm->cpu_vm_mask' and pass that copy down to the low-level flush
    routines after having tested that it was not empty.  But after changi=
ng
    it to just pass down the CPU mask pointer, the lower level TLB flush
    routines would now get a pointer to that 'mm->cpu_vm_mask', and that
    could still change - and become empty - after the test due to other
    CPU's having flushed their own TLB's.
   =20
    See
   =20
    	http://bugzilla.kernel.org/show_bug.cgi?id=3D13933
   =20
    for details.
   =20
    Tested-by: Thomas Bj=C3=B6rnell <thomas.bjornell-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w@public.gmane.org>
