Author: Eric Dumazet <eric.dumazet-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Thu Aug 6 15:09:28 2009 -0700

    execve: must clear current->clear_child_tid
   =20
    While looking at Jens Rosenboom bug report
    (http://lkml.org/lkml/2009/7/27/35) about strange sys_futex call done=
 from
    a dying "ps" program, we found following problem.
   =20
    clone() syscall has special support for TID of created threads.  This
    support includes two features.
   =20
    One (CLONE_CHILD_SETTID) is to set an integer into user memory with t=
he
    TID value.
   =20
    One (CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID) is to clear this same integer once the cre=
ated
    thread dies.
   =20
    The integer location is a user provided pointer, provided at clone()
    time.
   =20
    kernel keeps this pointer value into current->clear_child_tid.
   =20
    At execve() time, we should make sure kernel doesnt keep this user
    provided pointer, as full user memory is replaced by a new one.
   =20
    As glibc fork() actually uses clone() syscall with CLONE_CHILD_SETTID=
 and
    CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID set, chances are high that we might corrupt user
    memory in forked processes.
   =20
    Following sequence could happen:
   =20
    1) bash (or any program) starts a new process, by a fork() call that
       glibc maps to a clone( ...  CLONE_CHILD_SETTID | CLONE_CHILD_CLEAR=
TID
       ...) syscall
   =20
    2) When new process starts, its current->clear_child_tid is set to a
       location that has a meaning only in bash (or initial program) cont=
ext
       (&THREAD_SELF->tid)
   =20
    3) This new process does the execve() syscall to start a new program.
       current->clear_child_tid is left unchanged (a non NULL value)
   =20
    4) If this new program creates some threads, and initial thread exits=
,
       kernel will attempt to clear the integer pointed by
       current->clear_child_tid from mm_release() :
   =20
            if (tsk->clear_child_tid
                && !(tsk->flags & PF_SIGNALED)
                && atomic_read(&mm->mm_users) > 1) {
                    u32 __user * tidptr =3D tsk->clear_child_tid;
                    tsk->clear_child_tid =3D NULL;
   =20
                    /*
                     * We don't check the error code - if userspace has
                     * not set up a proper pointer then tough luck.
                     */
    << here >>      put_user(0, tidptr);
                    sys_futex(tidptr, FUTEX_WAKE, 1, NULL, NULL, 0);
            }
   =20
    5) OR : if new program is not multi-threaded, but spied by /proc/pid
       users (ps command for example), mm_users > 1, and the exiting prog=
ram
       could corrupt 4 bytes in a persistent memory area (shm or memory m=
apped
       file)
   =20
    If current->clear_child_tid points to a writeable portion of memory o=
f the
    new program, kernel happily and silently corrupts 4 bytes of memory, =
with
    unexpected effects.
   =20
    Fix is straightforward and should not break any sane program.
   =20
