Author: Xiao Guangrong <xiaoguangrong-BthXqXjhjHXQFUHtdCDX3A@public.gmane.org>
Date:   Wed Jul 15 12:29:06 2009 +0800

    tracing/function: Fix the return value of ftrace_trace_onoff_callback=
()
   =20
    ftrace_trace_onoff_callback() will return an error even if we do the
    right operation, for example:
   =20
     # echo _spin_*:traceon:10 > set_ftrace_filter
     -bash: echo: write error: Invalid argument
     # cat set_ftrace_filter
     #### all functions enabled ####
     _spin_trylock_bh:traceon:count=3D10
     _spin_unlock_irq:traceon:count=3D10
     _spin_unlock_bh:traceon:count=3D10
     _spin_lock_irq:traceon:count=3D10
     _spin_unlock:traceon:count=3D10
     _spin_trylock:traceon:count=3D10
     _spin_unlock_irqrestore:traceon:count=3D10
     _spin_lock_irqsave:traceon:count=3D10
     _spin_lock_bh:traceon:count=3D10
     _spin_lock:traceon:count=3D10
   =20
    We want to set _spin_*:traceon:10 to set_ftrace_filter, it complains
    with "Invalid argument", but the operation is successful.
   =20
    This is because ftrace_process_regex() returns the number of function=
s that
    matched the pattern. If the number is not 0, this value is returned
    by ftrace_regex_write() whereas we want to return the number of bytes
    virtually written.
    Also the file offset pointer is not updated in this case.
   =20
    If the number of matched functions is lower than the number of bytes =
written
    by the user, this results to a reprocessing of the string given by th=
e user with
    a lower size, leading to a malformed ftrace regex and then a -EINVAL =
returned.
   =20
    So, this patch fixes it by returning 0 if no error occured.
    The fix also applies on 2.6.30
   =20
    Signed-off-by: Xiao Guangrong <xiaoguangrong-BthXqXjhjHXQFUHtdCDX3A@public.gmane.org>
    Reviewed-by: Li Zefan <lizf-BthXqXjhjHXQFUHtdCDX3A@public.gmane.org>
